judge.go_no_go:
NO-GO on relaunching NOW. None of the five concepts has a decisive experiment that tests itself against the deployed arm-B pointer, and that pointer's own triad numbers at N=441/1105 are absent from the repo (its bake-off scripts are missing). Relaunch decisions should be gated on the following outcomes, in order.

(G0, required first, about 1 week) Run the in-harness arm-B pointer triad plus the SSOR, alias, CounterFact+ and powered format-transfer cohorts at N=441/1105, 3 reps. Every later bar is relative to these numbers.

(G1, Engram-site relaunch) Run Delta-Engram E0/E1 on Qwen3.5-4B with the NeuralDB cosine-gate control and the pointer in-run.
- GO for a relaunch around hashed residual rows only if all of these hold at some l <= 8, so that the Engram early-site identity survives:
  - paraphrase generality >= 0.82;
  - efficacy >= 0.88 at N=441 (3-rep mean, spread < 0.05);
  - efficacy(N=1105) - efficacy(N=137) >= -0.05;
  - same-subject other-relation agreement with the base >= 0.80;
  - CounterFact+ prefixed-neighbour keep within 0.02 of the base;
  - it beats arm B by >= 0.05 on the powered format-transfer/free-generation cohort or on span-exact for non-base-known multi-token objects;
  - deltas solved in HF bf16 survive the served 35B numerics in the parity probe, with solve time <= 10 s.
- If those hold only at l >= 16: ship it as a 'hashed-key NeuralDB' value option inside the pointer system, not as an Engram relaunch.
- If generality is < 0.70 at every l, or the arm wins only on the near-vacuous DEP-neighbour locality: the Engram site is dead on a frozen base. Close it and keep only the key-side components.

(G2, key front end) Adopt the hash-first + GTE-fallback (subject, relation) key as an evolution of arm B, never branded a relaunch, only if all of these hold:
- out-of-store false-fire < 0.05;
- alias recall on RippleEdits/UniEdit cohorts within 0.02 of whitened GTE;
- generality not below arm B;
- per-step trigger overhead <= a few percent of TPOT in the minisgl TP=2 spike, with no per-step host sync.
Otherwise keep arm B unchanged.

(G3, co-training / CF-Engram) Unfreeze only if both of these hold:
- G1 shows a per-fact frozen row stalls (solo < 0.88 at every depth);
- E1 shows raw-d rows stay flat in N where out_proj drops.
Then run a pre-registered late-port-only A1/A2/A3 contrast with >= 2 training seeds, thresholds re-measured on the retrofit base, Larimar/KBLaM reference arms, and ripple above the same-pass RAG oracle (0.346-0.531, RESULTS §10) on held-out relations as the primary pass bar. Without that ripple win, giving up the frozen-base invariant is not justified.

verdicts.objection:
Delta-Engram's main gain, efficacy that stays flat as N grows because there is no shared readout, is measured against the wrong incumbent. The 0.81 -> 0.565 drop belongs to the trained K1 + logit-inject arm (key-encoding §0(D), §6g). The deployed system is the whitened-GTE pointer, whose value is the exact object token-ids. Its delivery-exact equals its addressing by construction, and it forces tokens instead of asking the frozen circuit to overturn its prior (RESULTS §7, A/B/C bake-off; README "Where this is going"). So isolation buys nothing over what is already deployed.

Worse, moving the value back into a residual perturbation brings back the wall the deployed pointer sidesteps. The repo broke the ~0.7 residual wall down into (1) base-prior confidence (relation-level r=-0.60), (2) object-vocabulary size (r=-0.89) and (3) per-attempt noise (key-encoding §3.10-3.13). It showed the wall gets WORSE on a more knowledgeable base: Llama 0.574 vs Qwen 0.69 (§3.11). Those factors describe the frozen downstream circuit resolving a residual push against its own prior, not only the shared tap. They predict that Delta-Engram degrades exactly on the move to the 35B MoE the relaunch targets, while the pointer is immune to them.

The repo's closest per-fact, base-frozen, routed analogue is the distilled cartridge, a carrier with far more capacity than one d_model vector. It tops out at single-hop 0.84 and solo 0.90 (scorecard; RESULTS §10), which straddles the concept's own relaunch bar (efficacy >= 0.88) rather than clearing it. Caveat: that is a different base and task.

What would be uniquely better is two things: (i) no answer-span scoping and (ii) multi-token objects. The first is already largely solved: answer-span injection gives 12/12 and seed-once 9/12 (§3.20, §3.28). The second is close to untestable in the harness and walled for every continuous carrier. Multi-token supply is 2.7% of CounterFact (§3.19), and about 1/137 under the base-known filter (§3.28). Continuous values stall at t>=1: isolated per-token 0.50, store-forced/teacher-forced 0/4 (CAM_100_DISJOINT_RESULT). A pooled latent decoded "Normandy" as "Moscow" (§3.28).

Meanwhile the concept regresses the deployed system on four fronts:
- base-agnostic portability;
- surface-variant keys (the routed scorecard's 0.70 retrieval misses were aliases);
- synchronous writes (minisgl is inference-only, parity_spike.md §4(d));
- KV-cache invariance.

Its locality kill is near-vacuous for exact keys, because neighbours name other subjects. The real locality risk, essence bleed, has no repo anchor. Net: repo evidence does not make it clearly better than the pointer plus conf-gated logit inject. It is the prior assessment's unrun arm E0 recast as a thesis.

verdicts.salvage:
Do not relaunch the core. Run the training-free E0/E1 arm (engram-graft-assessment.md §3) against the actual deployed incumbent, the pointer id-bank (`_gen_ptr`, cam/recall_mag.py:2419, and `_iso_store_ok` hits_ptr), not only against A1 logit-inject. Compare at N=441/1105 with 3 reps, adding the new SSOR cohort to both arms.

Keep the pointer's lossless exact-id value and the logit/answer-span delivery. Adopt only the parts of the Engram design that repo evidence supports:
- the exact salted 2/3-gram hashed key as an alternative or secondary key to whitened-GTE;
- its exact presence/provenance bit, which feeds the +0.464 presence gate (RESULTS §8);
- CPU prefetch.

This is the 'arm D' fallback in the concept's own SITE KILL. Its Phase-0 CPU screen still applies:
- at most 5% out-of-store hit rate, against the pointer's 0.05;
- subject coverage after the frequency cut.

Consider a per-fact residual row only as an optional additive channel for the axis the pointer cannot serve, graded leans and relation-phrasing variants. Even then, require it to beat the pointer on SSOR-keep and on paraphrase generality at matched efficacy. Drop the per-base calibrated reader unless the uncalibrated per-fact row first beats 0.70 at ℓ≤8.
